<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Fitna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/680">680</a>–<a href="/years/692">692</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Fitna was a period of civil war in the Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> from 680 to 692. It began after the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-i">Yazid I</a> and included the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a> and the rival caliphate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> describe how the Marwanid branch of the Umayyads emerged victorious and consolidated the dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-fitna.jpg" alt="Second Fitna" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: original map by Al Ameer son; SVG version by Konstantinos Plakidas · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Second_Fitna_Territorial_Control_Map_ca_686.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The roots of the war lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-fitna">First Fitna</a>, which followed the killing of the caliph Uthman in 656. Mu'awiya I became sole ruler after the killing of Ali in 661 and the abdication of Ali's son Hasan, and in 676 he announced the nomination of his son Yazid as successor. With no precedent in Islamic history, hereditary succession aroused opposition, and it was regarded as turning the caliphate into a monarchy. The sons of several prominent companions of Muhammad, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</a>, objected, but Mu'awiya's threats and the general recognition of Yazid silenced them until Mu'awiya died in 680.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The war brought three challenges to Umayyad rule: Husayn and the pro-Alid movements, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Ibn al-Zubayr</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a>. Husayn, invited by supporters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, was intercepted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a> and killed in October 680. Yazid's Syrian army suppressed a rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in August 683 and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, where Ibn al-Zubayr had established himself, but the siege was abandoned after Yazid died in November. Most provinces then recognised Ibn al-Zubayr, while in parts of Syria Marwan I was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. The Penitents under Sulayman ibn Surad were crushed at Ayn al-Warda in January 685.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mukhtar-al-thaqafi">Mukhtar al-Thaqafi</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> in October 685, drawing support from non-Arab converts, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawali">mawali</a>, and his forces routed an Umayyad army at the Battle of Khazir in August 686, but he and his followers were killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zubayrids">Zubayrids</a> in April 687.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-ibn-marwan">Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan</a> reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> by defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zubayrids">Zubayrids</a> at the Battle of Maskin in 691, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Ibn al-Zubayr</a> was killed in the second siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 692. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a> had split into the moderate Najdat in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and the extremist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azariqa">Azariqa</a>, who sanctioned killing the women and children of their opponents. Organised Kharijite resistance collapsed by 692, with al-Muhallab's long campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> eliminating the Azariqa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The victory restored Umayyad authority and secured hereditary rule, and Abd al-Malik and his descendants held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> for another fifty-eight years, until the Abbasid revolution of 750. He centralised caliphal power, restructured the military and carried out policies of Arabization and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamization">Islamization</a> in the bureaucracy. The war also deepened the rivalry between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> and the Yaman among the Arab tribes. The death of Husayn is commemorated every year by Shi'a Muslims on the Day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a>, and the events of the war intensified sectarian tendencies from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shi'a denominations later developed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Fitna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His nomination of his son Yazid as successor led directly to the <b>Second Fitna</b> (civil war, 680–692).</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Second Fitna</b>, the Umayyad caliph Marwan I and his Kalb allies defeated the Qays tribes, who backed the Mecca-based caliph Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, north of Damascus.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-18">18 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Second Fitna</b>, Caliph Yazid I's Umayyad army under Muslim ibn Uqba fought the rebel defenders of Medina on the lava field of Harrat Waqim, north-east of the city.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-26">26 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The victory in the <b>Second Fitna</b> extended Mukhtar's rule over the Mosul region.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-6">6 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Second Fitna</b> he seized al-Yamama, his tribe's homeland in Najd, around 686 and was acknowledged as commander of the faithful by his followers.</blockquote><a href="/state/najdiyya">Najdiyya</a></li></ul></section>
